50,000 vectors of 512 numbers

brute force          12.3 ms per query   recall 100% by definition

M=8   ef_construction=40   built in   25.2s
     ef_search=10   recall@10  70.2%     0.08 ms     164x faster than exact
     ef_search=25   recall@10  70.6%     0.11 ms     109x faster than exact
     ef_search=50   recall@10  71.6%     0.17 ms      74x faster than exact
     ef_search=100  recall@10  73.6%     0.22 ms      56x faster than exact
     ef_search=200  recall@10  73.6%     0.38 ms      33x faster than exact

M=32  ef_construction=200  built in  341.6s
     ef_search=10   recall@10  96.4%     0.32 ms      39x faster than exact
     ef_search=25   recall@10  96.4%     0.18 ms      71x faster than exact
     ef_search=50   recall@10  96.8%     0.32 ms      38x faster than exact
     ef_search=100  recall@10  96.4%     0.40 ms      31x faster than exact
     ef_search=200  recall@10  96.2%     0.60 ms      21x faster than exact

ef_search is the dial. Larger explores more of the graph: slower, more accurate.
It is set per query, not at build time, so one index can serve a fast path and
an accurate one.

M and ef_construction are build-time, and changing them means rebuilding. Larger
M means more links per node: better recall, more memory, slower to build.
